WEBVTT

00:00:00.920 --> 00:00:03.250
With non-patterning knitting machines,

00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:07.690
and any time you want to work
a hand-manipulated stitch pattern,

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:11.930
there's usually a lot
of counting of needles involved.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:14.080
For simple stitch transfers,

00:00:14.150 --> 00:00:19.930
you can use a pencil or a water-soluble
pen and simply mark the needle bed.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:26.010
You could also use your needle
pushers to select sets of needles.

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:32.410
I found that cheat sheets come in handy
for more complicated stitch patterns.

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:37.410
We've created a set of sheets
for the various gauges of machines.

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:41.250
The grids are sized according
to the needle pitch.

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:45.490
That's the space between
the centers of the needles.

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:47.280
Here I'm using a 6.

00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:49.130
5 millimeter gauge,

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:55.640
and the needles of my LK150 fit
exactly in the boxes of the grid.

00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:00.650
I've marked my stitch
pattern sequence in pink.

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:03.960
I'll have two needles that will be small

00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:09.210
cables over three rows,
and there will be six needles

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:13.680
in between that are going
to remain in plain knitting.

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:17.520
As I'm knitting, I just simply line up

00:01:17.590 --> 00:01:21.450
the appropriate row on the cheat
sheet with my needle bed.

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:25.050
Pull out the needles
according to the chart.

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:28.770
I move the cheat sheet along the bed
for the width of my knitting,

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:35.370
and without having to count needles,
I'm now ready to do the hand manipulation.

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:39.130
Download a set of cheat sheets
from the Knit It Now website.

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:41.240
It includes all the common gauges

00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:44.680
of domestic knitting machines,
from fine gauge to Bulky

